Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: the walked constant-radius blend between two surfaces is a true
rolling ball (mitcad#121). Each section is the arc of the ball of the
radius touching both surfaces, in the plane through the ball's centre that
holds both contact normals (the normal plane of the curve the centre runs
along, the intersection of the surfaces' offsets).

Before, BlendFunc_ConstRad put both contact points in the plane normal to
the guide and took the centre along the surfaces' normals projected into
that plane. That is the rolling ball only where the normals are square to
the guide; elsewhere the "ball" is not at the radius from the surfaces (a
blend between a plate and a rod tilted by 40 degrees, radius 2, missed the
rolling ball by up to 0.014, and a rounding of a real part by 8 % of its
volume). The analytic blends (ChFiKPart) were rolling balls already and do
not change.

- BlendFunc_ConstRad: the equations are E(1) the mid-point of the contact
  points in the guide's normal plane (unchanged: it only says which ball
  goes with the guide's parameter) and E(2..4) pts1 - ray1 n1 = pts2 - ray2 n2
  with the surfaces' unit normals n1, n2 (the centre on both offsets).
  E(2..4) no longer depend on the guide, so their derivatives in t vanish;
  the derivatives in the surface parameters are those of the unit normal
  (first and second order). The sections (Section, the three variants for
  the approximation, and Tangent) are circles round that centre in the plane
  holding the directions from the centre to both contact points, oriented on
  the side of the guide's plane normal so that the arc runs as before; the
  plane's first and second derivatives come from those of the directions.
  Where the guide's normal plane holds both normals the result is the same
  as before; so a walked blend still meets an analytic one at a vertex.
- BlendFunc_ConstRadInv (the point where a contact reaches a restriction)
  solves the same equations, its contact on one surface held on the curve.
  Its Derivatives took the guide's plane derivative with a wrong term
  (theD for -nplan.d2); it now evaluates through Values.

Upstream: the change is local to the two functions; a user who relied on
the old sections could get them back behind a flag, but they are not a
rolling ball, which a constant-radius fillet is meant to be.
